remember evergrand in late september and  early october of last year they were the  star of financial news everyone was  worried about what would happen if this  massive chinese institution defaulted on  their debt and ripple effects that it  would have on the world we saw headlines  like fed warns china's property problems  could hurt global markets and the u.s  economy and wall street mark's biggest  drop since may as evergrain crisis  intensifies yet as the fed started  raising interest rates and the stock  market started to off for other reasons  it seems that most people have  completely forgotten about this once  monumental threat this isn't to say that  the evergreen situation can be  completely ignored though as the crisis  has only gotten worse  in fact at the end of last year  evergreen defaulted on 1.2 billion worth  of foreign bonds and as you would guess  their stock price has only fallen  further and further into the abyss  standing at just 17 cents today so  whatever happened to the ever grand  crisis and does evergreen even matter on  a world scale  before we get into the potential  consequences of the evergrand crisis  let's first take a look at how this  crisis even formed in the first place  this was not something that just took  place over a matter of years rather this  issue has actually been ruined for  decades going all the way back to the  1970s you see it in 1978 the chinese  government adopted a new economic policy  that many scholars call china's open  door policy before this ideology was  adopted china was completely closed off  to the world economy they practiced an  ideology called zhongju wei tia zhijuwe  yong which translates to chinese  learning as substance western learning  for application as the name suggests  china was intent on maintaining the  traditional culture as well as their  communist political and economic systems  however they were willing to import  technology from the west and implement  it into their society while this  strategy worked decently from much of  the 1900s it came with a slew of  shortfalls first of all china was barely  involved in foreign trade and secondly  no one wanted to invest in china which  made it increasingly difficult to keep  up with the west not to mention china's  economic and political landscapes had  been devastated by the cultural  revolution which took place between 1966  and 1976. so in 1978 china finally gave  in and adopted the open door policy  things didn't just change overnight  though china spent years deciding which  sectors they needed to control and which  sectors they could loosen up china was  by no means willing to give up land  ownership but they were willing to  privatize how the land was used and this  brings us to the amendment act of 1988  this amendment made it possible for  companies and individuals to use land to  their liking by obtaining land use  rights  this opened the door to a wide number of  entrepreneurs who were looking to build  real estate and property development  companies and as you would guess  african's founder zuzian fell under this  category zoo was actually a bit late to  this trend as he didn't start evergreen  until 1996 which is eight years after  the amendment was adopted  nonetheless evergrande would leverage  this new opportunity to the max and the  company would grow exponentially over  the next two decades effortless growth  was only further accelerated by china  joining the world trade organization in  2001 this made chinese investments more  appealing than ever before which allowed  evergreen to grow an international base  of investors and stakeholders  every grand actually went public during  the height of the great financial  recession in october of 2009 but despite  this they were still able to raise 722  million dollars which just goes to show  how much interest there was in chinese  real estate amongst international  investors all of these factors would  allow evergrand to become one of the  largest property developers in china and  one of the biggest companies in the  world  in fact the company was and still is  part of the global 500 coming in at rank  122. this monumental growth that didn't  come for free though evergrand was  constantly taking on more and more risk  which would eventually come back to  haunt them  one of the biggest risks of the chinese  real estate market over the past two  decades has been their ever-growing real  estate bubble one of the most famous  examples of this is the dozens of ghost  cities located across china  if you're not familiar with china's  ghost cities these are essentially  massive developments that never became  inhabited  times you see dozens of skyscrapers and  buildings that are completely empty and  sometimes not even finished  now you might be wondering how in the  world do these ghost cities even form in  the first place well if we take a look  at chinese real estate law we'll quickly  begin to understand how exactly this  happened china separates real estate  development into a few different  categories starting with economically  affordable housing china requires that  such projects are occupied by the owner  and these projects cannot be bought or  sold as an investment on top of this  real estate companies cannot sell these  properties for more than five percent  over the construction cost so these  projects aren't particularly lucrative  or appealing one category that is super  appealing though is commodity housing  which can be bought and sold as an  investment in most cases and in china  there's extraordinary demand for real  estate real estate is often thought of  as the best way to grow generational  wealth so even many middle class  families opt to buy second and third  properties as soon as they can afford it  also china has yet to experience a 2000  sale housing market collapse so as far  as the everyday chinese person knows  real estate prices only go up and  honestly this is not a bad bet  even if there is a crash it's just a  matter of time until real estate  recovers because people will always need  land but here's the problem you can't  own land in china so if you want a piece  of real estate in an area that you think  will develop in 20 or 30 years you have  to build something on it right now and  when millions of chinese people are  thinking the same thing it doesn't take  long to garner enough demand to build an  entire go city so while these cities are  indeed mostly if not completely empty  they're also completely sold out this  has provided chinese real estate  developers with unprecedented demand but  when all of your demand comes from  investors and not people who are looking  for a primary residence it just takes  one market downturn to obliterate your  demand  but wait a minute there's no way the  chinese government didn't see what was  happening right how did they not think  to stop this unsustainable practice  well the answer is twofold first of all  local governments are super incentivized  to sell land rights to these developing  companies you see municipal governments  only get to keep about 40 percent of  their country's tax revenue the rest  must be passed on to the ccp however  local governments have to pay for 80 of  their expenses so local governments have  to do something to overcome this funding  disparity and the easiest answer for  them has been selling land rights in  2015 for instance land transferring fees  accounted for 40 to 60 percent of local  government revenue this is not that  surprising when you consider that land  sales brought in 438 billion for local  governments in 2012. i don't think i  need to explain why local governments  were a fan of ghost cities but what  about the ccp  surely they could see that this was a  massive bubble waiting to blow up right  well they did and they would eventually  take action but not before letting the  situation get too far out of hand you  see it the ccp was also massively  incentivized to turn a blind die to  these ghost cities the ccp wasn't  directly pulling in cash from land sales  like local governments but they were  able to pump up the chinese gdp  the chinese real estate industry  accounts for 29 of the entire country's  gdp  so letting these ghost cities pop up was  extremely beneficial in terms of  catching up and beating the us in terms  of gdp at first this wasn't a big issue  as real state developers were pretty  responsible financially but it didn't  take long for greed to kick in for  developers like evergrand they started  taking out substantially more debt and  their debt equity ratio fell off a cliff  for example in 2014 evergrand had 112  billion won in equity and 206 billion  won in assets this gave them an  extremely healthy debt to equity ratio  of 0.839 meaning that they had more  equity than debt by 2020 their equity  had grown to 350 billion yuan which  sounds good until you realize that their  assets have grown to 2.3 trillion yuan  this results in a super dangerous debt  to equity ratio of 5.57  if evergrand was an average american  they would have had to take off pmi or  private mortgage insurance because they  didn't even have 20 equity this issue  wasn't just limited to evergrand either  many chinese real estate companies were  in similar positions which made it  abundantly clear to the ccp that they  had to take action  in august of 2020 chinese regulators met  with the country's largest real estate  companies including evergrand and they  introduced three restrictions called the  three red lines these restrictions put  caps on three debt ratios which were  debt to cash debt to equity and debt to  assets as you would guess afrogrand did  not exactly fall within these  restrictions so they would start selling  off properties and raising cash for  example in september of 2020 they  discounted properties by 30 percent to  increase sales similarly in october they  raised 555 million by completing a share  offering they also cancelled certain  projects but none of this was enough to  improve their financial situation  substantially nonetheless african was  able to stay afloat thanks to sufficient  cash flow but it didn't take long for  this to go downhill as well  in late 2020 jack ma and the ccp would  get into a bit of a conundrum which  prompted the ccp to crack down on  chinese tech companies while most of the  ccp's actions were directly targeted  towards tech companies as tech stocks  crumbled so did investor confidence and  as investors started pulling back on  real estate investment africa's net  profit started to decline as well in  august of 2021 africa reported 29  year-over-year decline in net profit and  given evergrand's already fragile  position this was enough for default  fears to go rampant african continued to  insist that while they were facing  issues there was nothing to worry about  you could say that these were their  famous last words as evergrand would  miss their first set of coupon payments  just a few weeks later shortly after a  program would begin missing coupon  payments on a regular basis until they  eventually defaulted on 1.2 billion  dollars worth of debt  the ccp has never publicly acknowledged  that they're billing out evergrand but  that's basically what they've been doing  they're not exactly saving the company  itself but they're trying their best to  ensure that the collapse of evergrand  does not lead to further consequences  and the main way they're accomplishing  this is by nationalizing the company for  example local governments have been  seizing rights granted to evergrand  similarly china's central bank freed up  188 billion dollars to help alleviate  any liquidity problems caused by  evergren's default evergrand has also  been selling parts of their own business  to raise cash and to pay off debt so at  this point it does look like china has  evergrand under control and that they'll  be able to contain a crisis but only  time will tell do you think the  evergreen crisis is behind us or is it  just a preview as to what's to come  comment that down below  also drop a like if you think that  china's ghost cities are spooky and of  course consider joining our discord  community to suggest future video ideas  and consider subscribing and see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one  you
 

